e when the ground war  began  it would be unfair to say that there was  no fighting because  it would suggest that the soldiers  didn't have a job to do which is wrong  they did i think they did it  extraordinarily well but there was an  awful lot more surrendering  than there was fighting most iraqis gave  up as soon as they could  and to anyone they could give up to tell  the story when you came upon  you're in your  nissan  truck or was it  four-wheel drive  and you came upon the  uh iraqi  soldiers  that would have been the second i guess  day of the ground war and or the third  and dan and i were trying to get to  kuwait city because it was clear that  the war was for all practical purposes  over  and we wanted  if you remember it only lasted 100 hours  we wanted to get to kuwait city uh for  the liberation  um  we were dr we were trying to make our  way up a highway that ran inside iraq  ultimately to kuwait city  and  picking our way through it slowly  because the iraqis had  set dynamite charges  in places across the roads to to stop  you from going forward and because there  were mine fields all around and not all  of them were marked and you had to sort  of watch your step  and  we it was dusk and it was raining and it  was miserable and we came suddenly upon  a group of  a wretched looking men  holding waving a standing in the middle  of the road waving a a white flag  a t-shirt on a piece of bamboo i think  and they were clearly iraqis and they  were unarmed and they were  they cut a pathetic site i mean they  they were desperate to to surrender  because it was getting on tonight it was  freezing cold and it was raining and i  don't think any of them had eaten in  some time  and they were  afraid that if they stayed out on the  battlefield somebody might mistake them  for  something people worthy of killing  so they  they asked to surrender to us and  we didn't we didn't want to do it i mean  we were we tried to tell them we were  not in that line of work that we were  just the two of you yeah give an  interpreter with you this time no and  they sort of only one of them spoke  english and was very little bit but it  was clear  and we  fumbled our way  i think at least for myself in a state  of mild embarrassment through this  um  exchange where  we were  like people trying to beg off from a  dinner party invitation we didn't want  any part of it we just wanted to  leave them alone we gave them a lot of  our food  and orange juice and things like that  but we didn't want to take prisoners  because it seemed  it wasn't our role i mean we were not in  in the prisoner taking business  uh  so we pushed on and  went on a little further but were turned  back by a a  minefield that had not been cleared  um by the way just uh i kept asking  myself when i read your book how did you  fuel where'd you get we would uh i i  bought uh we bought in kuwait city  before we set out  15 big jerry cans  at the hardware store or something like  that and filled them up with gasoline  and carried  um  we carried them with us in the back of  the uh thing  at all times um so you know anywhere  between 10 and 15  jerry ken's filled up with gas um so  anyway you pushed on you had to found  the minefield and then you turned around  turned back because it was getting dark  and if the minefield wasn't cleared we  were not going to try to get across it  and dark with uh  i guess work and  came back and saw the  poor guys still trudging along in the  rain and it was just too miserable to  leave them there so  we took them not as prisoners but more  as taxi drivers  i mean we we were  not uh this was not an act of of uh  subjugation on our part we were merely  escorting them uh  and  they climbed on top of the car and in  the car and so on and we get them a  little farther on  and to give them to the first army unit  which we we ran across which was a saudi  brigade supply brigade that had not seen  any action and  they went  uh bananas over the prospect of of  capturing the enemy and  um  went around jamming clips into their  rifles and  yelling and so on and terrifying the  kuwaitis who thought that they were all  going to be shot in the sand and so the  kuwaiti i mean the iraqis so the  iraqis are crying and carrying on and  you know praying and the saudis are  yelling and  dan and i are standing there  bemused at the side  and  not knowing i suppose i didn't know what  to  do  but it  it devolved very quickly into something  that wasn't again sort of  not like what you think of a surrender  in a time of war the saudis had no real  interest in  hurting the iraqis there there was  always among the arab troops a sense of  kinship  with the iraqis a sense that they were  after all islam and arabic and that they  should not  be killed the egyptian army for instance  went to i think considerable pains from  what i saw to avoid killing iraqis  they would not  they were they would wait and let people  come to them and surrender rather than  take a building by storm and so on  there's one point though you uh  said that you saw one of the saudi  soldiers go over and give the iraqi a  kiss  yes at the very end uh  which uh  i i suppose was a signal to all of us  that nothing bad was going to happen uh  the  the saudis calmed saudi soldiers calmed  down a bit and as they calmed down the  iraqis started to calm down and one of  them went up and there was one iraqi who  was sort of weeping and clutching at  himself and he gave him a kiss i think  on each cheek and then everybody sort of  relaxed  you went to kuwait  i went to kuwait uh how long did it take  you by the way from that time not too  long we the next morning we went back to  that highway and uh the minefield had  had been cleared in the early morning  by i suppose american and perhaps  egyptian troops and there was a path  through it and that was the last  minefield between us and kuwait city so  we were able  to get to kuwait city by about noon of  that day uh and  uh get past a few checkpoints into the  city itself  time has gone by fast but some things  that people there's left for people to  read in this book you spend how much  time in in kuwait i spent about two or  three weeks i think  in kuwait and in the environs around  kuwait up to the iraqi border and so on  and  up the highways where the iraqi army had  retreated uh  and had been uh  if you remember  the roads where they were blown up and  so on in retreat uh about two weeks  mostly one copy back at this time there  was in kuwait uh  a fairly  fairly quickly  enough in the way of communications that  you could dictate  uh things two or three news  organizations at least no more than that  probably six or seven bought in  satellite phones all the networks had  one  and the boston globe  for whom i was stringing had  half share in a satellite phone  with a time magazine i think  and i think maybe one other news  organization so you could get a  satellite phone out in the evening what  are people at the boston globe and the  new republic and places like that  telling you why you're over there what  is it they like  what is it they want more of  you couldn't get a there wasn't enough  time to have long conversations about a  lot of stuff but i think  uh most of the reporting that was done  in kuwait  city uh  was  i mean to some degree self-evident you  come into a city where  that has been occupied by an army and is  now liberated then the you know you  there are  you're going to cover two things one the  story of the liberation and two the  story of what it had been like under  occupation so every day you just go out  and  do a different facet of that story is  there any one piece that you wrote while  you're over there for any of these  publications that got the most feedback  i think probably the uh  i wrote a piece uh  just before i left kuwait city about  the emir's new palace for the new  republic that  uh to the  effect that the u.s army corps of  engineers was involved in the business  of refurbishing  along extremely swank lines uh a palace  for the emir who was coming home to  kuwait after sitting out the war and  luxury digs elsewhere  and you know the the spectacle of  the army corps of engineers supervising  workers doing things like putting fresh  silk up on the walls and  installing new gold faucets for the  bathrooms in a city where people were  still  without food or water and so on  i think got a lot of attention you also  write some about sanctions did they work  no i don't think they did i don't think  they ever would have  i think that  the smuggling uh  links which have now been amply proven  between jordan and iraq  would have always kept enough  going  that the country  could not be forced into  into compliance with the united nations  well by sanctions alone  mostly because the people who run iraq  saddam hussein and his uh  his  fellow bath party  leaders and military leaders and so on  they run a gangster state and which they  control absolutely and the notion that  the sanctions might cause tremendous  suffering to the people of iraq which  they did and indeed still do  is not i don't think a tremendous  concern  the people who run iraq the men who want  run iraq still eat quite well and drink  quite well and have mistresses and  very nice mercedes that they took from  kuwait and so on in their life it's not  that painful  white house correspondent for the new  york times is that the pinnacle  i don't know i never thought of that is  it i often you always want it yes sure  why not  what's it mean what kind of a job is it  um  it's uh  it's a fun job right now this is an  i suppose a  one of the best of all times to be  watching a new administration since this  administration is trying to do so much  so quickly  there's a there is a sense every day i  think for most people in  in  our line of work in washington that  you're here at a time  where there is an awful lot of  substantive change about to happen or  in the midst of happening and that that  is that is thrilling get any sense of  what the white house  thinks of you  no i don't uh have any great sense but i  don't really i suppose look for any  great sense of it  any sense of what you think of them i'm  still in a process of learning about  them i don't really  i mean  they are a work in progress  i don't try not to form any  final  conclusions about them or anybody else i  cover on a day-to-day  basis people treat you differently now  that you're in this job  um  i don't know uh  my mother doesn't  not at all or my wife so  i don't i don't think too much now your  father is a  reporter for the washington times  you say he influenced you to get into  this business tremendously what was it  like growing up in the family it was  great fun we grew up on capitol hill and  my  parents ran a uh  i suppose a kind of typical newspaper  family house and there were always  people over for dinner and  there was always lots of  commotion at the dinner table and lots  of people arguing about  the events of the day and  a lot of excitement in the air i i grew  up  watching  grown-ups  argue around the dinner table at great  length about things that they were not  particularly well informed on which is  excellent training for her life in  washington  what'd your mom do my mom is also a  writer she writes about child care for  a the washington post and other  newspapers she has a syndicated column  and writes books on child care also  what's her column called uh her the  family almanac  marguerite kelly  and she wrote  she writes books called the mother's  almanac and mother's almanac too  and after a year or so in this book by  the way is there going to be a paperback  on it yes there is  what's the best thing that people will  say about this book from your standpoint  i would be happy if they said that the  writing was good i  i wanted to write something that was not  not a journalistic account as much as it  was  a piece of writing i wanted to write  something that  would use writing to look at the way war  affects what happens in times of war not  the gulf war but war in general and the  way people are and the way they behave  i'd be very happy about that  this is what the book looks like our  guest has been michael kelly the author  it's all about his  trips over to the middle east during the  gulf war it's called martyr's day  chronicle of a small war thank you very  much for joining us thanks for having me  michael kelly was killed thursday night  while covering the war in iraq he was  embedded with the army's third infantry  division and was killed in a humvee  accident he's the first american  journalist to die in the current  conflict he was 46 years old  you
 

